{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head f2f45497404c67eb883222edbf098b869b3472d7.\n\nConditions met, in the order requested. docs/getting-started.md carries `gs init` in a runnable `sh` block with its operator argument, and the page now runs top to bottom to a working workroom rather than only built binaries. It states what init creates and what it leaves alone: no branch written, no HEAD moved, no index or working tree touched, because the genesis tree comes from `git mktree`, which never reads the index. The block is `sh`, so the docset example gate executes it — verified by the gate passing on the changed page. The page rests_on the artifact for the init behaviour it describes (8345229e3d, the instance-identity merge that made the operator named and required) alongside its existing basis, not on the request alone.\n\nTwo corrections to my own earlier account, found by re-deriving the claims at this head instead of reusing the assert I filed before the work. First, init does not create only a parentless empty-tree commit: it creates that genesis and then appends the bootstrap roster event, so refs/seq/\u003cgenesis\u003e points at the roster event, not at the genesis. The page says so. Second, \"no default identity\" is true of the CLI, not of the package — app.Init falls back to the name \"operator\" when passed an empty string, and cmd/gs never lets that path run because --operator or GITSEQ_ACTOR is required. The page describes the CLI, which is what a reader meets. I also added that the operator role carries ratifier with it, which the page did not say and a first-time operator needs.\n\nTests: go vet clean; make build; make test green across all sixteen packages including internal/docset at 101.9s, run over the final tree rather than an earlier one. Not run: the UI gates, which this change cannot reach — two markdown files, no code and no embed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fc8354d5d47b7